
========================================================================
A  exact odd-prime Paley law: tournament (p=3 mod4) vs graph (p=1 mod4)
========================================================================
  p= 3 (3 mod4): TOURNAMENT (p=3 mod4); regular (all out-degree 1)? True
  p= 5 (1 mod4): GRAPH sym (p=1 mod4); regular (all out-degree 2)? True
  p= 7 (3 mod4): TOURNAMENT (p=3 mod4); regular (all out-degree 3)? True
  p=11 (3 mod4): TOURNAMENT (p=3 mod4); regular (all out-degree 5)? True
  p=13 (1 mod4): GRAPH sym (p=1 mod4); regular (all out-degree 6)? True
  => 3,7,11 (=3 mod4) are TOURNAMENTS; 5,13 (=1 mod4) are self-complementary GRAPHS.
     p=2 is outside this odd-prime Paley law; no 'i*sqrt(2)' claim is made.

========================================================================
B  tournament spectrum: a shifted half-scale of the Gauss sum, plus the principal eigenvalue
========================================================================
  p= 3: char_A = (x-1)(x^2+x+1)^1 ? True; quad disc = -3 = -p ; roots (-1+-i*sqrt3)/2
  p= 7: char_A = (x-3)(x^2+x+2)^3 ? True; quad disc = -7 = -p ; roots (-1+-i*sqrt7)/2
  p=11: char_A = (x-5)(x^2+x+3)^5 ? True; quad disc = -11 = -p ; roots (-1+-i*sqrt11)/2
  Paley-3: x^2+x+1 = Phi_3 (primitive cube roots); Paley-7: (-1+-i*sqrt7)/2 (THM-1830);
  Paley-11: (-1+-i*sqrt11)/2. Nonprincipal eigenvalues are (-1 +- g_p)/2, not g_p itself.

========================================================================
C  the quadratic GAUSS SUM g_p = sum legendre(a,p) zeta_p^a : |g_p|^2=p ; i*sqrt p (3mod4) vs sqrt p (1mod4)
========================================================================
  p= 3: g_p = +0.0000+1.7321i ; |g_p|^2 = 3.0000 = p? True ; IMAG i*sqrt p (=3 mod4)
  p= 5: g_p = +2.2361-0.0000i ; |g_p|^2 = 5.0000 = p? True ; REAL sqrt p (=1 mod4)
  p= 7: g_p = +0.0000+2.6458i ; |g_p|^2 = 7.0000 = p? True ; IMAG i*sqrt p (=3 mod4)
  p=11: g_p = +0.0000+3.3166i ; |g_p|^2 = 11.0000 = p? True ; IMAG i*sqrt p (=3 mod4)
  p=13: g_p = +3.6056+0.0000i ; |g_p|^2 = 13.0000 = p? True ; REAL sqrt p (=1 mod4)
  => 3,7,11 have g_p=i*sqrt(p); 5,13 have g_p=sqrt(p). Adjacency eigenvalues are shifted half-scales.
     For Paley graphs the separate principal adjacency eigenvalue is (p-1)/2.

========================================================================
D  heuristic small-prime comparisons for LRC(14): arithmetic checks, no implication
========================================================================
  7 (apex, 14=2*7): x^14-1 == (x-1)^7 (x+1)^7 (mod 7)? True  [THM-2043 F_7[C_14]=F_7[X]/(X-1)^7 x F_7[X]/(X+1)^7]
  7 cap field Q(cos 2pi/7): 2cos(2pi/7)=1.24698 root of x^3+x^2-2x-1? True (disc 49=7^2, cubic)
  3 comparison: Phi_6(14)=14^2-14+1=183 => t*=14/183; Paley-3 has Phi_3, with Phi_6(x)=Phi_3(-x)
  5 comparison: Q(sqrt5), phi=1.61803, x^2-x-1=0 -> Fibonacci/golden-field analogy (S206)
  11 comparison: rank ker_Z(1,...,12)=11; multiples of 11 in [1,14] = [11]; neither fact forces an LRC speed

========================================================================
SUMMARY -- exact Paley kernel and limits of the LRC analogy
========================================================================
  EXACT: for odd prime p, the quadratic-residue relation is a tournament for p=3 mod4 and a graph
  for p=1 mod4. Its nonprincipal adjacency spectrum is (-1 +- g_p)/2, while (p-1)/2 is principal.
  OUTSIDE SCOPE: p=2 is not covered. Phi_3 (Paley-3) and Phi_6 (the cited LRC parameter) differ by x -> -x.
  HEURISTIC ONLY: the labels 2/chirality, 3/argmax, 5/foil, 7/apex, and 11/rank are comparison prompts.
  No Paley object, field match, scarcity observation, or equal rank by itself implies an LRC(14) fact.
